handbook.markdown:
The best-practices page says Genie uses Unity Catalog column names and descriptions to generate responses, and the concepts page says it selects relevant names and descriptions from annotated tables and columns. Descriptions are therefore part of the model's input, not documentation for humans only.

A good table description says what one row is. A good column description says what is counted, in which unit, with which exclusions, and how the column relates to the metric contract:

```sql
COMMENT ON TABLE quality_pilot.accepted.daily_line_rate IS
  'One row per North plant line per business day, from accepted inspections only (quarantined and withheld excluded).';
ALTER TABLE quality_pilot.accepted.daily_line_rate
  ALTER COLUMN defective_units COMMENT 'Physical units found defective at inspection. Numerator of unit_defect_rate.';
ALTER TABLE quality_pilot.accepted.daily_line_rate
  ALTER COLUMN inspected_units COMMENT 'Physical units inspected; voided inspections carry zero. Denominator of unit_defect_rate.';
ALTER TABLE quality_pilot.accepted.daily_line_rate
  ALTER COLUMN restated COMMENT 'True when a later correction replaced the value for this day; the previous value is kept.';
```

What to inspect: for each word in the benchmark's questions, whether some description contains it or a synonym. 'Rejects' appearing nowhere is a prediction that 'rejects' questions will be guessed. Renaming columns helps more than describing them when consumers allow it, and descriptions must agree with the metric contract and with example SQL; a comment that says 'rate, 2 decimals' beside an example that rounds to one teaches two rules. Descriptions do not enforce anything: they help interpretation choose, and the benchmark checks whether it chose well.

This case uses a standalone Genie Agent. Its saved author credentials provide warehouse access; query data access uses the asker, including row filters and column masks. Editing also requires SQL entitlement, data SELECT, warehouse CAN USE and agent CAN EDIT. Autogenerated dashboard companions instead follow dashboard credentials, which can use the publisher's data grants. Linked agents keep their own credential settings.

Cinderline's grants, from the access review:

```sql
GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG quality_pilot TO `north-reporting-analysts`;
GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA quality_pilot.accepted TO `north-reporting-analysts`;
GRANT SELECT ON TABLE quality_pilot.accepted.daily_line_rate TO `north-reporting-analysts`;
-- No grant on quality_pilot.quarantine for analysts.
```

`SELECT` alone is not enough: the asker also needs `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` on the parents, which is why the review grants all three.

Review sharing, query permissions and curated context separately. Format-assistance samples are selected with author permissions and shared as agent context. Inspect that context as well as grants and the test identity when investigating a disclosure; per-user query enforcement alone does not certify the context. Run audience-specific checks before rollout.
